#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:22
msgid "4. PyGMT I/O: Table inputs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:24
msgid ""
"Generally, PyGMT accepts two different types of data inputs: tables and "
"grids."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:26
msgid ""
"A table is a 2-D array with rows and columns. Each column represents a "
"different variable (e.g., *x*, *y* and *z*) and each row represents a "
"different record."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:28
msgid ""
"A grid is a 2-D array of data that is regularly spaced in the x- and y-"
"directions (or longitude and latitude)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:31
msgid ""
"In this tutorial, we'll focus on working with table inputs, and cover grid "
"inputs in a separate tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:34
msgid ""
"PyGMT supports a variety of table input types that allow you to work with "
"data in a format that suits your needs. In this tutorial, we'll explore the "
"different table input types available in PyGMT and provide examples for "
"each. By understanding the different table input types, you can choose the "
"one that best fits your data and analysis needs, and work more efficiently "
"with PyGMT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:42
msgid ""
"from pathlib import Path\n"
"\n"
"import geopandas\n"
"import numpy as np\n"
"import pandas as pd\n"
"import pygmt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:55
msgid "ASCII table file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:57
msgid ""
"Most PyGMT functions/methods that accept table input data have a ``data`` "
"parameter. The easiest way to provide table input data to PyGMT is by "
"specifying the file name of an ASCII table (e.g., ``data=\"input_data."
"dat\"``). This is useful when your data is stored in a separate text file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:64
msgid ""
"# Create an example file with 3 rows and 2 columns\n"
"data = np.array([[1.0, 2.0], [5.0, 4.0], [8.0, 3.0]])\n"
"np.savetxt(\"input_data.dat\", data, fmt=\"%f\")\n"
"\n"
"# Pass the file name to the data parameter\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 5], projection=\"X10c/5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(data=\"input_data.dat\", style=\"p0.2c\", fill=\"blue\")\n"
"fig.show()\n"
"\n"
"# Now let's delete the example file\n"
"Path(\"input_data.dat\").unlink()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:83
msgid ""
"Besides a plain string to a table file, the following variants are also "
"accepted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:85
msgid "A :class:`pathlib.Path` object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:86
msgid ""
"A full URL. PyGMT will download the file to the current directory first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:87
msgid ""
"A file name prefixed with ``@`` (e.g., ``data=\"@input_data.dat\"``), which "
"is a special syntax in GMT to indicate that the file is a remote file hosted "
"on the GMT data server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:91
msgid ""
"Additionally, PyGMT also supports a list of file names, :class:`pathlib."
"Path` objects, URLs, or remote files, to provide more flexibility in "
"specifying input files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:97
msgid "2-D array: `list`, `numpy.ndarray`, and `pandas.DataFrame`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:99
msgid "The ``data`` parameter also accepts a 2-D array, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:101
msgid "A 2-D :class:`list` (i.e., a list of lists)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:102
msgid "A :class:`numpy.ndarray` object with a dimension of 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:103
msgid "A :class:`pandas.DataFrame` object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:105
msgid "This is useful when you want to plot data that is already in memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:109
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 5], projection=\"X10c/5c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Pass a 2-D list to the 'data' parameter\n"
"fig.plot(data=[[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], style=\"c0.2c\", fill=\"black\")\n"
"\n"
"# Pass a 2-D numpy array to the 'data' parameter\n"
"fig.plot(data=np.array([[4.0, 2.0], [6.0, 4.0]]), style=\"t0.2c\", "
"fill=\"red\")\n"
"\n"
"# Pass a pandas.DataFrame to the 'data' parameter\n"
"df = pd.DataFrame(np.array([[7.0, 3.0], [9.0, 2.0]]), columns=[\"x\", "
"\"y\"])\n"
"fig.plot(data=df, style=\"a0.2c\", fill=\"blue\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:131
msgid ":class:`geopandas.GeoDataFrame`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:133
msgid ""
"If you're working with geospatial data, you can read your data as a :class:"
"`geopandas.GeoDataFrame` object and pass it to the ``data`` parameter. This "
"is useful if your data is stored in a geospatial data format (e.g., GeoJSON, "
"etc.) that GMT and PyGMT do not support natively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:140
msgid ""
"# Example GeoDataFrame\n"
"gdf = geopandas.GeoDataFrame(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"geometry\": geopandas.points_from_xy([2, 5, 9], [2, 3, 4]),\n"
"        \"value\": [10, 20, 30],\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# Use the GeoDataFrame to specify the 'data' parameter\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 5], projection=\"X10c/5c\", frame=True)\n"
"fig.plot(data=gdf, style=\"c0.2c\", fill=\"purple\")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:161
msgid "Scalar values or 1-D arrays"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:163
msgid ""
"In addition to the ``data`` parameter, some PyGMT functions/methods also "
"provide individual parameters (e.g., ``x`` and ``y`` for data coordinates) "
"which allow you to specify the data. These parameters accept individual "
"scalar values or 1-D arrays (lists or 1-D numpy arrays)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:170
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 5], projection=\"X10c/5c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Pass scalar values to plot a single data point\n"
"fig.plot(x=1.0, y=2.0, style=\"a0.2c\", fill=\"blue\")\n"
"\n"
"# Pass 1-D lists to plot multiple data points\n"
"fig.plot(x=[5.0, 5.0, 5.0], y=[2.0, 3.0, 4.0], style=\"t0.2c\", "
"fill=\"green\")\n"
"\n"
"# Pass 1-D numpy arrays to plot multiple data points\n"
"fig.plot(\n"
"    x=np.array([8.0, 8.0, 8.0]), y=np.array([2.0, 3.0, 4.0]), "
"style=\"c0.2c\", fill=\"red\"\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:193
msgid "Conclusion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/04_table_inputs.rst:195
msgid ""
"In PyGMT, you have the flexibility to provide data in various table input "
"types, including file names, 2-D arrays (2-D :class:`list`, :class:`numpy."
"ndarray`, :class:`pandas.DataFrames`), scalar values or a series of 1-D "
"arrays, and :class:`geopandas.GeoDataFrame`. Choose the input type that best "
"suits your data source and analysis requirements."
msgstr ""

